WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:03.128
<v Examiner>Sperm, eggs and ovulation.

2
00:00:03.128 --> 00:00:04.119
<v Examiner>The fine print.

3
00:00:04.569 --> 00:00:08.594
The written tracks give you gamete production and the two cycles.

4
00:00:08.594 --> 00:00:12.618
Her quizzes ask the same ground in matching tables, cell by cell,

5
00:00:12.618 --> 00:00:14.290
with the chromosome counts.

6
00:00:14.290 --> 00:00:15.961
This track is those tables.

7
00:00:16.411 --> 00:00:18.326
The hook: head, midpiece, tail.

8
00:00:18.326 --> 00:00:22.342
The acrosome breaks in, the mitochondria pay, and the tail swims.

9
00:00:22.792 --> 00:00:25.138
The sperm cell, her six-blank table.

10
00:00:25.138 --> 00:00:28.917
The head holds the twenty-three chromosomes, as chromatin,

11
00:00:28.917 --> 00:00:34.065
and it's capped by the acrosome, which is full of enzymes to penetrate the egg.

12
00:00:34.065 --> 00:00:38.886
Her case: a man whose sperm are healthy, swim straight, and are plentiful,

13
00:00:38.886 --> 00:00:40.646
but can't penetrate an egg.

14
00:00:40.646 --> 00:00:43.643
The acrosome is short of the enzymes it needs.

15
00:00:43.643 --> 00:00:47.096
The midpiece is packed with mitochondria, the engine:

16
00:00:47.096 --> 00:00:52.635
they turn the fructose from the seminal vesicles into A T P, the energy for swimming,

17
00:00:52.635 --> 00:00:55.372
so faulty mitochondria mean poor motility.

18
00:00:55.372 --> 00:00:57.391
And the tail propels the sperm.

19
00:00:57.391 --> 00:00:59.802
Short, double or crooked tails can't.

20
00:00:59.802 --> 00:01:05.145
Her true or false: sperm carry a lot of food in their heads, for the long journey.

21
00:01:05.145 --> 00:01:05.536
False.

22
00:01:05.536 --> 00:01:08.143
Their fuel is the fructose in the semen.

23
00:01:09.593 --> 00:01:13.691
The cells, with their chromosome counts, her matching table.

24
00:01:13.691 --> 00:01:16.696
The spermatogonium is the diploid stem cell,

25
00:01:16.696 --> 00:01:20.930
and it divides by mitosis into a diploid primary spermatocyte.

26
00:01:20.930 --> 00:01:23.594
That's why males make gametes for life.

27
00:01:23.594 --> 00:01:27.009
Meiosis one gives haploid secondary spermatocytes,

28
00:01:27.009 --> 00:01:29.809
and meiosis two gives haploid spermatids.

29
00:01:29.809 --> 00:01:33.429
Spermiogenesis turns the spermatids into spermatozoa.

30
00:01:33.429 --> 00:01:39.439
Her multiple choice version: spermatogonium, diploid spermatocyte, haploid spermatocyte,

31
00:01:39.439 --> 00:01:41.078
spermatid, spermatozoon.

32
00:01:41.078 --> 00:01:47.020
And inhibin, from the Sertoli cells, slows sperm production when the count is too high.

33
00:01:47.470 --> 00:01:49.117
Oogenesis, her diagram.

34
00:01:49.117 --> 00:01:54.702
Oogonia are diploid cells that divide by mitosis into diploid primary oocytes,

35
00:01:54.702 --> 00:01:57.280
and that only happens in fetal life.

36
00:01:57.280 --> 00:02:02.937
Her true or false: oogonia are present in the ovary from menarche to menopause.

37
00:02:02.937 --> 00:02:03.366
False.

38
00:02:03.366 --> 00:02:07.949
The primary oocytes form before birth, and wait in prophase one.

39
00:02:07.949 --> 00:02:12.961
Meiosis one gives a haploid secondary oocyte and the first polar body.

40
00:02:12.961 --> 00:02:18.117
The secondary oocyte is released at ovulation, waiting in metaphase two.

41
00:02:18.117 --> 00:02:22.413
Meiosis two is completed at fertilisation, in telophase two,

42
00:02:22.413 --> 00:02:25.277
giving the ovum and a second polar body.

43
00:02:25.277 --> 00:02:28.929
The polar bodies are haploid cells that degenerate.

44
00:02:29.379 --> 00:02:30.878
Her six differences.

45
00:02:30.878 --> 00:02:37.475
Duration: spermatogenesis is continuous from puberty to death, and oogenesis is monthly,

46
00:02:37.475 --> 00:02:39.498
from menarche to menopause.

47
00:02:39.498 --> 00:02:43.771
Cytokinesis: the sperm cells divide with equal cytoplasm,

48
00:02:43.771 --> 00:02:49.692
and the oocyte divides unequally, into one large oocyte and small polar bodies.

49
00:02:49.692 --> 00:02:54.115
The result: four motile sperm, against one non-motile ovum.

50
00:02:54.115 --> 00:02:59.062
And her false statement: both involve meiosis followed by mitosis.

51
00:02:59.062 --> 00:02:59.512
False.

52
00:02:59.512 --> 00:03:01.985
It's mitosis first, then meiosis.

53
00:03:03.435 --> 00:03:05.068
Ovulation, her drop-down.

54
00:03:05.068 --> 00:03:08.071
A secondary oocyte is released from the ovary.

55
00:03:08.071 --> 00:03:10.618
Ovulation is triggered by a peak in LH.

56
00:03:10.618 --> 00:03:14.927
The ovarian wall ruptures when the fluid-filled Graafian follicle,

57
00:03:14.927 --> 00:03:18.257
which she also calls the tertiary follicle, bursts.

58
00:03:18.257 --> 00:03:21.456
And the force of the fluid pushes the oocyte out,

59
00:03:21.456 --> 00:03:24.199
toward the fimbriae of the fallopian tube.

60
00:03:24.199 --> 00:03:27.202
The fimbriae sweep over the ovary to catch it,

61
00:03:27.202 --> 00:03:30.793
and the cilia in the tube move it on toward the uterus.

62
00:03:31.243 --> 00:03:32.538
Her hormone tables.

63
00:03:32.538 --> 00:03:37.104
FSH, from the pituitary, is highest during the proliferative phase,

64
00:03:37.104 --> 00:03:39.081
and makes the follicles grow.

65
00:03:39.081 --> 00:03:44.260
Oestrogen, released by the developing follicle, peaks just before ovulation,

66
00:03:44.260 --> 00:03:46.646
and stimulates the secretion of LH.

67
00:03:46.646 --> 00:03:50.735
LH, from the pituitary, peaks highest just before ovulation.

68
00:03:50.735 --> 00:03:54.824
Progesterone, released by the corpus luteum after ovulation,

69
00:03:54.824 --> 00:03:59.867
dominates the secretory phase, and develops the glands of the endometrium.

70
00:03:59.867 --> 00:04:03.752
Oestrogen and progesterone both peak in the luteal phase.

71
00:04:03.752 --> 00:04:06.478
And one of her keys to learn as written:

72
00:04:06.478 --> 00:04:11.657
LH stimulates the production of progesterone by the follicle's thecal cells.

73
00:04:12.107 --> 00:04:12.846
The traps.

74
00:04:12.846 --> 00:04:15.209
Oogonia only exist before birth.

75
00:04:15.209 --> 00:04:17.203
It's mitosis, then meiosis.

76
00:04:17.203 --> 00:04:21.633
The egg is released as a secondary oocyte, in metaphase two.

77
00:04:21.633 --> 00:04:26.507
And the acrosome gets the sperm in; the mitochondria make it swim.

78
00:04:26.957 --> 00:04:27.591
<v Examiner>Once more.

79
00:04:27.591 --> 00:04:29.240
<v Examiner>Sperm, eggs and ovulation.

80
00:04:31.690 --> 00:04:37.517
The sperm head holds twenty-three chromosomes as chromatin, capped by the acrosome,

81
00:04:37.517 --> 00:04:39.763
whose enzymes penetrate the egg.

82
00:04:39.763 --> 00:04:46.011
The midpiece mitochondria turn fructose into A T P for swimming, and the tail propels it.

83
00:04:46.011 --> 00:04:48.679
Sperm don't carry food in their heads.

84
00:04:48.679 --> 00:04:54.295
A diploid spermatogonium divides by mitosis into a diploid primary spermatocyte,

85
00:04:54.295 --> 00:04:58.928
then meiosis gives haploid secondary spermatocytes and spermatids,

86
00:04:58.928 --> 00:05:01.526
and spermiogenesis makes spermatozoa.

87
00:05:01.526 --> 00:05:04.896
Inhibin slows production when the count is high.

88
00:05:04.896 --> 00:05:07.985
Oogonia divide by mitosis only before birth.

89
00:05:07.985 --> 00:05:12.337
Meiosis one gives a haploid secondary oocyte and a polar body,

90
00:05:12.337 --> 00:05:15.356
and meiosis two completes at fertilisation.

91
00:05:15.356 --> 00:05:17.041
Polar bodies degenerate.

92
00:05:17.491 --> 00:05:22.841
Spermatogenesis is continuous from puberty to death, with equal cytoplasm,

93
00:05:22.841 --> 00:05:24.866
and gives four motile sperm.

94
00:05:24.866 --> 00:05:30.939
Oogenesis is monthly, menarche to menopause, unequal, and gives one non-motile ovum.

95
00:05:30.939 --> 00:05:33.325
It's mitosis first, then meiosis.

96
00:05:33.325 --> 00:05:38.314
At ovulation, the LH peak bursts the Graafian, or tertiary, follicle,

97
00:05:38.314 --> 00:05:42.002
and the secondary oocyte is pushed to the fimbriae.

98
00:05:42.002 --> 00:05:45.038
FSH is highest in the proliferative phase.

99
00:05:45.038 --> 00:05:49.087
Oestrogen peaks just before ovulation and stimulates LH.

100
00:05:49.087 --> 00:05:52.196
Progesterone dominates the secretory phase.

101
00:05:52.196 --> 00:05:56.390
Her key: LH stimulates progesterone from the thecal cells.
